From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] cts: clustering's min-cost flow costs are int64_t
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Clustering::minCostFlow costs a sink-to-mean arc at the distance to the
power of the clustering power (4) and keeps the arc while that is below
INT_MAX, so a cost reaches 2^31 - 1. LEMON's network simplex ran with int
costs and starts the node potentials at half the cost type's range plus
one, 2^30 for int. A reduced cost, an arc's cost plus the potential of
one end minus the other, could then lie outside int and wrap: an arc that
raises the cost looked like one that lowers it, and the simplex took it.

On a large hierarchical design (XiangShan's XSTile parent, in
bazel-orfs), CTS never returned: H-tree level 5 of a clock net with 6,663
sinks after sink clustering, one 394-sink refinement. Replayed alone,
that min-cost flow did not return in 20 s with int costs: 4.3e9 reduced
cost comparisons, 1.0e8 of them decided otherwise than in exact
arithmetic. With exact arithmetic and int's 2^30, or with int64_t costs,
it returns in under a millisecond with the same flow. Halving its costs
to below 2^30 makes int return that flow too.

The costs are now int64_t and the potentials start at 2^62; no sum of
costs a potential collects comes near the type's range. The arcs and
their costs are the same. What else changes is that artificial cost:
with int, routing a sink whose cheapest augmenting path costs more than
2^30 (one arc, or a chain that moves other sinks between the means) was
dearer than leaving its unit on the artificial arc, and Kmeans's
nearest-cluster fallback placed the sink instead; now the flow routes it.
Where no such path exists and nothing wraps, the flow is the same.

Six of cts's regression goldens change for that reason and are not
updated here: array, array_ins_delay, array_max_wl (and
array_max_wl.defok), array_no_blockages, array_repair_clock_nets and
max_wl_two_sinks. In array: 904 clock buffers instead of 906, average
sink wire length 4,274.45 um instead of 4,227.48 um.

Cluster.CostsNearIntMaxDoNotWrap fails without the change (sinks 3 and 4
swap means; the wrap alone does it, exact arithmetic with int's 2^30
gives the right answer) and passes with it.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires with the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
---
 src/cts/src/Clustering.cpp   | 10 ++++++++--
 src/cts/test/cts_unittest.cc | 37 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 45 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cts/src/Clustering.cpp b/src/cts/src/Clustering.cpp
index 67484e6..b112c81 100644
--- a/src/cts/src/Clustering.cpp
+++ b/src/cts/src/Clustering.cpp
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <cfloat>
 #include <cmath>
+#include <cstdint>
 #include <cstdio>
 #include <cstdlib>
 #include <ctime>
@@ -332,7 +333,12 @@ void Clustering::minCostFlow(const std::vector<std::pair<float, float>>& means,
              countArcs(graph));
 
   // formulate min-cost flow
-  ListDigraph::ArcMap<int> edge_cost(graph), edge_capacity(graph);
+  // The costs are int64_t: an arc costs up to INT_MAX, and the network
+  // simplex starts node potentials at half the cost type's range. With int
+  // costs a reduced cost could fall outside int and wrap; the simplex then
+  // took arcs that raise the cost, and could cycle forever.
+  ListDigraph::ArcMap<int64_t> edge_cost(graph);
+  ListDigraph::ArcMap<int> edge_capacity(graph);
   for (auto& edge : src_sink_edges) {
     edge_capacity[edge] = 1;
   }
@@ -363,7 +369,7 @@ void Clustering::minCostFlow(const std::vector<std::pair<float, float>>& means,
     debugPrint(logger_, CTS, "clustering", 2, " cap = ", edge_capacity[it]);
   }
 
-  NetworkSimplex<ListDigraph, int, int> flow(graph);
+  NetworkSimplex<ListDigraph, int, int64_t> flow(graph);
   flow.costMap(edge_cost);
   flow.upperMap(edge_capacity);
   flow.stSupply(src, target, means.size() * cap + remaining);
diff --git a/src/cts/test/cts_unittest.cc b/src/cts/test/cts_unittest.cc
index d43b741..43251ed 100644
--- a/src/cts/test/cts_unittest.cc
+++ b/src/cts/test/cts_unittest.cc
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
 // Copyright (c) 2023-2025, The OpenROAD Authors
 
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <utility>
 #include <vector>
 
@@ -72,6 +73,42 @@ TEST(Cluster, BranchingPointIsDisabledIfNoBranchesAreProvided)
   EXPECT_NE(means_without_branching[0].first, means_with_branching[0].first);
 }
 
+// Three sinks next to each mean and room for three in each cluster: each
+// sink joins the mean it is next to. A sink's cost to a mean is the
+// distance to the fourth power, up to INT_MAX: up to 2.1e9 to the far mean
+// here. LEMON's network simplex starts node potentials at half the cost
+// type's range, 2^30 for int; with int costs a reduced cost fell outside
+// int and wrapped, the simplex took arcs that raise the cost, and sinks 3
+// and 4 swapped means. On a large design it never returned.
+TEST(Cluster, CostsNearIntMaxDoNotWrap)
+{
+  utl::Logger logger;
+  CKMeans::Clustering clustering(/*sinks=*/{{6.0, -10.1},
+                                            {201.8, -25.1},
+                                            {-7.9, -18.5},
+                                            {22.5, -1.5},
+                                            {198.3, 4.3},
+                                            {202.4, 2.2}},
+                                 &logger);
+  std::vector<std::pair<float, float>> means = {{0, 0}, {188.4, 0}};
+  // One assignment to these means, no further iterations.
+  clustering.iterKmeans(/*iter=*/1,
+                        /*n=*/2,
+                        /*cap=*/3,
+                        /*max=*/0,
+                        /*power=*/4,
+                        means);
+
+  std::vector<std::vector<unsigned>> clusters;
+  clustering.getClusters(clusters);
+  ASSERT_EQ(clusters.size(), 2);
+  for (auto& cluster : clusters) {
+    std::sort(cluster.begin(), cluster.end());
+  }
+  EXPECT_EQ(clusters[0], (std::vector<unsigned>{0, 2, 3}));
+  EXPECT_EQ(clusters[1], (std::vector<unsigned>{1, 4, 5}));
+}
+
 // Check that we avoid a divide-by-zero error when the region has
 // zero height.
 TEST(SinkClusteringTest, ZeroHeightRegion)
